WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.020
Digital technologies and social media 

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.700
have fundamentally reshaped

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.020
 political and civic participation.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.620
The public, especially 
the younger generation,

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.580
demand greater involvement
 in decision-making.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.900
They want a chance 
to shape policy 

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.420
and in a way that goes beyond 

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.780
voting in elections
 every 5 years.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.980
The European Parliament,
 the Council 

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.900
and the European Commission
 have heard this call.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.340
Our response is the Conference
 on the Future of Europe.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:32.820
The Conference is a citizen-focused,
 bottom-up democratic exercise.

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.260
It gives people the chance to influence
 how the EU works for them.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:41.580
A key feature of the Conference
 will be the European Citizens’ Panels.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.860
800 Europeans from the 27 EU Member States
 are randomly selected.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.860
They are sorted into four panels
 of 200 people each –

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.300
Each panel is representative
 of the EU’s diversity 

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.700
in terms of age, gender,
geographic origin, 

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:01.420
level of education
 and socio-economic background. 

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:07.020
Young people between 16 and 25 will make up 
one third of each panel. 

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.940
Then they will get to work 
discussing the big topics 

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.060
that EU citizens care about.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.250
These topics will be chosen by citizens –

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.060
an innovative multilingual 
 digital platform 

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:22.180
has been set up to gather views.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.620
People around Europe can submit 
and debate ideas 

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.740
and issues using the platform –

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.540
and the outcomes will inspire
 the discussions of the panels.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.000
With the assistance of facilitators 
and expert insights, 

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.650
the panels will meet
 and deliberate together.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:44.140
This will take place in 24 EU languages, 
during three sessions of two days each.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.060
The panel members will be asked
 to work together, 

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.460
discuss, and ultimately 
form recommendations.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.220
Representatives from each panel 
will then present the outcome 

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.180
of their discussions to the Plenary, 

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.580
 and debate them 
with other participants.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.780
And then the EU institutions
 will need to follow up

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.060
This is the commitment we have made.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.900
This is one of the most ambitious 

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:09.660
citizens’ consultation exercises
 ever undertaken.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:14.780
The EU is embarking on an exciting path 
of true democratic innovation 

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.580
aimed at strengthening our
 representative democracy –

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:24.300
and these multilingual, pan-European panels
 are one of the most exciting parts of it!

